sity anisotropy into \(p_{\rm T}\) anisotropy quantified by \(v_{n}\). For example, a study in 2019 found that a third to a half of \(v_{2}\) in heavy-ion collisions may result from kinematics requiring one mean free path of interaction length in a non-hydrodynamized system [54].

While investigations into \(v_{2}\) from initial collision stages and hadronic interactions remain an ongoing and nuanced work, \(v_{2}\) remains a strong signal for QGP formation in heavy-ion systems. A particularly convincing signal is the \(v_{2}({\rm KE}_{\rm T})\) correlation per particle type as shown in Figure 1.19. Here, \({\rm KE}_{\rm T}\equiv\sqrt{p_{\rm T}^{2}+m^{2}}-m\) which is, at mid-rapidity and to leading order, equal to \(E_{\rm K}\), a universal scaling parameter for \(v_{2}\) in perfect fluid dynamics [57, 58]. This mass ordered scaling, and universal scaling, is apparent from left to right in each of the two panels. The scaling by number of quarks (\(n_{q}\)) strongly suggests that the elliptic flow occurred at a quark level in a QGP which then hadronized with co-moving quarks in phase space into mesons and baryons. If the flow occurred at a molecular hadron level, then baryons would be boosted to comparable speeds as mesons on the same \(v_{2}\) distribution and the hadrons' curves would move to the right of, and stay below, the mesons' curves, instead of exhibiting the crossover presented by the data.

