### 5.7 MC and Phase Space Suppression

PYTHIA8 has been used to model \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\,\)GeV \(p+\)Au collisions, the results of which are clustered into anti-\(k_{\rm T}\) jets with \(R=0.4\). The simulation quantified \(\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}\) as the total number of charged particles produced in each collision in the acceptance of STAR's BBC. A plot of this simulated \(\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}\) vs the leading (highest \(p_{\rm T}\)) jet in the \(|\Delta\phi|>(7/8)\pi\) bin is shown in Figure 5.12.

The simulated value for ratio of EA-binned semi-inclusive jet spectra is:

\[\frac{S_{\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm{High}}}}{S_{\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm{Low}}}}= \frac{N_{\rm{jets}_{\rm BBC}}^{\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm{High}}}}{\frac{N_{\rm{ jets}_{\rm{jets}}}^{\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm{Low}}}}{N_{\rm{triggers}}^{\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm{Low}}}}}= \frac{N_{\rm{triggers}_{\rm{triggers}}}^{\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm{High}}}}{N_{ \rm{triggers}}^{\rm{EA}_{\rm{jets}}^{\rm{High}}}} \tag{5.12}\]

Figure 5.12: PYTHIA8 simulation of \(p+\)Au \(\sqrt{s_{\rm NN}}=200\,\)GeV events. Top panel: The number of particles the Au-going BBC acceptance is defined as the simulated \(\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}\). The \(N_{\rm part}\) for the \(32^{\rm th}\) and \(63^{\rm rd}\) percentiles are shown plotted (it wasn’t possible to get exactly one-third of the distribution in either bin because of the integer count definition of the EA) as defined for all events are shown. The x-axis is the leading \(p_{\rm T}\) full jet. The bottom panel shows the ratio of events for each jet-\(p_{\rm T}\) bin that are in the \(\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm{High}}\) and \(\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm{Low}}\) bins.

The EA definition is for all events, as mentioned above. The ratio of triggers is:

\[\frac{N_{\mathrm{triggers}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{Low}}}}{N_{ \mathrm{triggers}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{High}}}}=\frac{32\%}{27 \%}\approx 1.2. \tag{5.13}\]

In the simulation, we have the luxury of binning each event by the leading recoil jet value, and plotting only the entry for the lead recoil jet such that there is exactly one entry per trigger and jet. Therefore, that ratio of 1.2 is also the ratio of the total number of jets \(>8\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) in the low to high \(\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}\) bins:

\[\frac{S_{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{High}}}}{S_{\mathrm{ EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{Low}}}}\Bigg{|}_{\mathrm{All\;jets}}=\frac{N_{ \mathrm{triggers}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{Low}}}}{N_{\mathrm{ triggers}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{High}}}}\frac{N_{\mathrm{ jets}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{Low}}}}{N_{\mathrm{jets}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{ \mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{Low}}}}=1.2\times\frac{1}{1.2}=1.0 \tag{5.14}\] \[\mathrm{with}\;E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{trig}}\in[8,30]\,\mathrm{ GeV}/c\;\&\;p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{jet}}>8\,\mathrm{GeV}/c \tag{5.15}\]

However, one can see by eye, that the simulation has a negative correlation between the average lead recoil jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) and the EA. At \(p_{\mathrm{T,jet}}\approx 30\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) the ratio of events (and/or jets) is about 1.4, such that:

\[\frac{S_{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{High}}}}{S_{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{ BBC}}^{\mathrm{Low}}}}\Bigg{|}_{\mathrm{Jets\;with}\;p_{\mathrm{T}}\approx 30\, \mathrm{GeV}/c}=\frac{N_{\mathrm{triggers}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{ \mathrm{Low}}}}{N_{\mathrm{triggers}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{ High}}}}\frac{N_{\mathrm{jets}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{High}}}}{N_{ \mathrm{jets}}^{\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}^{\mathrm{Low}}}}=1.2\times\frac{1} {1.4}=0.86 \tag{5.16}\]

Hence, the PYTHIA8 simulation predicts an anti-correlation between \(\mathrm{EA}_{\mathrm{BBC}}\) and jets at mid-rapidity, and a consequent suppression of \(\sim\)14% of \(30\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) jets recoiling from a trigger that turns on with \(8\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) recoiling jets.

### 5.8 Measurement Indications of Jet Suppression

Before presenting the jet spectra measurements in the following chapter (Chapter 6), it is already clear that some jet spectra suppression to EA correlation is to be expected from the EA-to-trigger correlation, independent of any actual possible jet quenching. Just how much suppression is expected? It depends on the ratio of each jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) to each trigger \(E_{\mathrm{T}}\). From the qualitative arguments made above, strong suppression is expected for harder jets mixed with softer triggers.

The ratios of total number of full jets produced per number of triggers in high EA to low EA events are presented in Figure 5.13 (using EA\({}_{\rm BBC}\)) and Figure 5.14 (using EA\({}_{\rm TPC}\)). Each figure has four sub panels, each for a separate \(10\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) sub set of the overall jet range of [10,50]\(\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\). The color in each panel represents the overall enhancement or suppression of jets per trigger, in which the trigger range runs from a lower bound (indicated by the x-axis location of the bin) to the upper bound (indicated by the y-axis location of the bin).

The relative distribution of jets among an overall set of ranges from the lower bound to the maximum upper bound (\(30\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\)) is indicated by circles (squares) in each bin for high (low) EA. The larger the circle or square, the higher percentage of the jets within that bin. (For example, the bin at (\(E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB}\in[14,15]\) & \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm UB}\in[18,19]\)) has a circle (square) whose size represents the number of jets from events with triggers in the range [18,19]\(\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) divided by those in the entire column (i.e. jets from events with triggers in the range [14,30]\(\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\)).

Triggers in the range \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}>25\,\mathrm{GeV}\) have been omitted from Figures 5.14 and 5.13 to simply allow more room to display the information in the range \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\in[8,25]\,\mathrm{GeV}\). As shown in Figure 5.7, there are very few triggers with \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}>25\,\mathrm{GeV}\) such that the omission of these few high \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\) events on the plots is not significant.12

Footnote 12: Besides which, it is evident in the figures themselves that the suppression is already stable by the upper bound of \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}=25\,\mathrm{GeV}\) when using a lower bound less than about \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\approx 18\,\mathrm{GeV}\). Also refer to Figure 5.15.

The expectation presented in argument in Section 5.6 is that the EA suppression of hard jets should result from having a large trigger range \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\in[E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB},E_{\rm T}^{\rm UB}]\), and that the high-EA events simply starve the population of hard triggers (and therefore indirectly hard jets) near the upper bound (\(E_{\rm T}^{\rm UB}\)) relative to the much larger population of jets near the lower bound (\(E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB}\)). In this scenario, there should be stronger suppression for hard jets for larger ranges of \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\) (i.e. the phase space represented by bins higher-above the diagonal turn-on where \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm UB}=E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB}+1\,\mathrm{GeV}\)). The data plotted in Figures 5.13 & 5.14 only partially support this theory. It is true that higher suppression occurs for harder jets in conjunction with softer triggers. However, it is not generally shown that for a given low-value \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB}\) that raising the value of \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm UB}\) appreciably increases the suppression ratio of hard jets. Instead,the suppression is already largely determined by the first bin on the diagonal, and doesn't get stronger moving up from the diagonal.

A plot of the values of the first column in each of the quadrants in Figures 5.13 & 5.14 is given in Figure 5.15, in which the spectra are clearly not universally falling for all the harder jets. The falling trend fits best for jets at, using.

