### 6.9 Semi-Inclusive Acoplanarity

Jet modification of a QGP medium can be probed not only via \(p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{jet}}\), but also by deflection of the dijet azimuth's from being directly back-to-back at exactly \(|\Delta\phi|=\pi\). Broadening of large angle scattering (acoplanarity) beyond the amount normally produced from initial and final state radiation (ISR and FSR) is a proposed signature of a QGP due to scattering of individual particles in the QGP (Moliere scattering) [122]. The same jets used to probe the EA dependence of the semi-inclusive \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) spectra (\(S\)) reported in Sections 6.2-6.7 are used to present \(\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}\)10 binned measurements of \(|\Delta\phi|\) spectra.

Footnote 10: \(\rm{EA}_{\rm TPC}\) is not used for the obvious reason that it is defined by the activity at \(|\Delta\phi|\in[(3/8)\pi,(5/8)\pi]\).

The effects of combinatoric jets from UE activity are mitigated in two ways. First, only jets with \(p_{\rm T}>10\,\rm{GeV}/c\) are used; as seen in Figure 6.2 there is very little background at those energies. Second, jets from a second set of collisions with a softer trigger tower requirement, \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\in[4,6]\,\rm{GeV}\), are also measured. The same UE jet objects should be equally present for harder and for softer triggers.11 Therefore, if the soft-trigger acoplanarity spectra is subtracted from the hard-trigger acoplanarity spectra, the UE should be removed, and whatever remains should be associated with a hard trigger.12

Footnote 11: The underlying event, UE, is by definition the activity not associated with the hard scattering.

The \(|\Delta\phi|\) distributions are plotted in Figure 6.8, binned by \(\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm High}\) and \(\rm{EA}_{\rm BBC}^{\rm Low}\), and also by events with \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\in[4,6]\,\rm{GeV}\) and \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\in[8,30]\,\rm{GeV}\). The distributions are of full jets with \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}>10\,\rm{GeV}/c\) and labelled as \(A\left(|\Delta\phi|\right)\).

In order to quantify the broadening of the distribution, the cumulative distribution (CD)

