## Chapter 7 Summary and Conclusion

### 7.1 Summary

In the last few years, the position of small system collision studies within experimental and theoretical high energy physics has evolved dramatically. Within the context of probing the QGP in A+A collisions via jets, a relatively simple paradigm using the optical limit in the Glauber model, with \(\langle N_{\rm part}\rangle\) EA scaling and \(\langle N_{\rm coll}\rangle\) jet scaling, was a powerful method to sort out QGP signatures from the complex signals that are A+A collision measurements. However, application of the same methodology to \(p/d\)+A collisions through inclusive measurements at ATLAS [34] and PHENIX [72], and then subsequent semi-inclusive measurements by ALICE [75, 76] presented surprising results, which motivated, and contextualized, the results presented in this thesis.

To leading order in \(p\)+A collisions, jets may be associated with the Bjorken-\(x\) of the hard scattered parton in the proton as \(x_{\rm p}\approx 2E_{\rm jet}/\sqrt{s}\)[123]. These studies reveal that \(x_{\rm p}\) of the jets is a useful metric in comparing results at different energy scales. This separates the ALICE measurements, whose maximum recorded charged jet \(p_{\rm T}\) values are at a little under \(x_{\rm p}\approx 0.1\); from ATLAS which reaches values on the order of \(x_{\rm p}\sim 0.4\), and RHIC measurements at PHENIX and STAR at \(x_{\rm p}\sim 0.5\).

In small system collisions, the previous results indicate:

1. In the measurements of high \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\) jets, when not binned by EA, the overall jet production is consistent with Glauber scaled \(pp\) values.
2. At high \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\), jet spectra EA dependence appears to scale monotonically with increasing jet \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\)[34].
3. At low \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\) values, the measured semi-inclusive and inclusive jet spectra has had no measurable EA dependence [75, 76].
4. If the low \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\) jet quenching limit (from ALICE) is extended to the high \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\) range, and jet quenching is assumed to be the cause of the modification, then a tension with the ATLAS results is observed [75].

The STAR results presented in this thesis are the first semi-inclusive, small system jet results, at RHIC kinematics (and therefore, also at high \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\)). They show a significant suppression of the semi-inclusive jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) spectra, \(S\) binned by high-EA events relative to \(S\) binned by low-EA events. It is demonstrated that this EA dependence does not result from a trivial autocorrelation in which the jet activity itself (or lack of it) directly influences the EA measurements. Rather, it is shown that at high \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\) values, there is a very definite correlation between EA and \(E_{\mathrm{T}}^{\mathrm{trig}}\), and therefore presumably \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\). This complicates the question of asking about jet modification via EA itself, but also indicates that this is a good opportunity to use these \(p\)+A collisions to better probe the initial stages of the collision itself. This may be done through looking for the mechanism that (anti)correlates EA with \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\). Some possibilities are:

* Perhaps the \(x_{\mathrm{p}}\)-to-EA correlation is a result of fluctuating proton configurations. Such initial state fluctuations are already of interest to potentially explain collective motion signals in small systems (see Figure 1.18, also [124]). They may also explain the EA to jet-\(x_{\mathrm{p}}\) dependence. If the presence of a high-\(x_{\mathrm{p}}\) parton reduces the proton cross section, such as by decreasing the average number of other partons available for interactions, then the \(\langle N_{\mathrm{part}}\rangle\) in \(p\)+A collisions would decrease in correlation with the presence of higher \(x_{\rm p}\) partons [125]. EA scales with the softer \(\langle N_{\rm part}\rangle\), and thereby be anti-correlated with the \(x_{\rm p}\) scale of jet production.
* Perhaps it is a result energy conservation constraints on the successive collisions of the high-\(x_{\rm p}\) proton with the nucleons in the heavy ion. How does the energy depletion affect the wave function and how is that communicated? Is there perhaps a kind of color-force transparency with results similar to a shrinking proton [126]?

Because the EA-to-\(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\) anticorrelation is observed, then high-to-low EA \(S\) suppression is expected and, as reported in this thesis, is observed. The degree of suppression on the azimuthal side of the event recoiling from the trigger appears to scale monotonically with \(p_{\rm T}\) and therefore \(x_{\rm p}\).1

Footnote 1: Note that the conversion between \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) and \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) for the jets in this thesis is approximately linear. The maximum correction factor at the boundary of the jet rapidity acceptance \(\eta_{\rm jet}=\pm 0.6\) is \(\sim\)1.19.

Studies of the relationship between \(p_{\rm T}^{\rm jet}\) range, \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\) range, and EA induced semi-inclusive spectra suppression have also been presented. The naive picture that \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\in[E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB},E_{\rm T}^{\rm UB}]\) should monotonically result in more suppression of hard jets with a fixed \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB}\) and increasing \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB}\) is not observed.2 The argument was that the suppression should occur because the EA-correlation is strongest with higher \(x_{\rm p}\) triggers toward \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm UB}\), but the spectra are normalized by the dominant number of softer triggers from events toward \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm LB}\). The discrepancy is likely due to the steeply falling spectrum of the triggers, such that even soft-triggers produce a dominant number of the higher \(x_{\rm p}\) jets, thereby minimizing the \(E_{\rm T}^{\rm trig}\) range's effect. This effect could be better probed by replacing the trigger towers with full jets whose clustering includes the trigger towers; this would remove some of the smearing between trigger and jet spectra.3

Footnote 2: Specifically, see Figures 5.13, 5.14, 5.15.

Footnote 3: See Figure 6.10 for an indication of the degree of this smearing.

The results have also been used to check for semi-inclusive acoplanarity broadening. The study reveals a slight broadening within about 0.4 radians, peaking at about 0.2 radians. The \(S\) spectra dependency on EA is expected as a direct result of the EA-to-trigger cor relation because of the per-trigger normalization. The acoplanarity result is a jet shape distribution with no such direct dependency on per-trigger normalization. As such, the expected relationship to the EA-trigger bias is not clear and this result is a promising venue for further study. While these measurements are not corrected for detector efficiency, there is very little background, particularly for jets with \(p_{\mathrm{T}}>10\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\), and the JES and JER are relatively independent of EA. As such, results in ratio to EA are not anticipated to change.

Finally, the dijet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) balance is reported at high and low EA (as measured by both the BBC and TPC). The measurements are normalized per dijet, and are thereby not influenced by any per-trigger normalization bias from the EA-to-\(x_{\mathrm{p}}\) correlation. The results see no EA-dependency on the dijet balance. They indicate the EA-modification of the \(S\) spectra does not result from jet quenching, but instead results from correlations formed prior to jet formation. This interpretation is reinforced by recent STAR measurements of the \(p\)+Au jet mass distribution, conducted concurrently with this research, as shown in Figure 7.1. There, jet mass is defined as \(M\equiv|\Sigma_{i\in\mathrm{jet}}p_{i}|=\sqrt{E^{2}-\mathbf{p}^{2}}\) where \(\mathbf{p}\) is the three momentum of the jet [127] and is sensitive to jet substructure evolution and possible interaction with a QGP. As seen, there is no EA dependence on the \(M\) distribution. As in the case with the acoplanarity measurements, the \(A_{\mathrm{J}}\) results are not corrected for detector effects; however, the same arguments for these jets at \(10\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) and \(20\,\mathrm{GeV}/c\) apply as for the acoplanarity measurement. Therefore, the EA ratios of the results are not anticipated to change.

### 7.2 Conclusion and Outlook

These measurements at STAR show that EA-to-trigger correlations result in significant EA correlation in per-trigger normalized jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) spectra. Per-dijet normalized observables, such as the dijet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) balance reported in this thesis, and the jet mass distribution concurrently measured at STAR and recently reported, show no EA dependence. This suggests that the EA-to-trigger correlation is fully responsible for the EA dependency of the jet spectra, and that there is no quenching of jet \(p_{\mathrm{T}}\) by a QGP in \(p\)+Au collisions after jet formation. The small but observable broadening of the azimuthal distribution of jets away from the triggersin high-EA events cannot be trivially explained in terms of the EA-to-trigger correlation, could possibly result from jet-medium interactions which are too weak to quench jet \(p_{\rm T}\), remain an intriguing result that is not fully understood, and worthy of further investigation.

For reasons reported in this thesis, it is an exciting time to be studying jets in small systems in high energy physics. The field's understanding of initial collisions in these systems is evolving quickly, and jets are a central feature of that field. As shown in this thesis, while phase space constraints of EA to \(x_{\rm p}\) correlations cannot be ignored when interpreting small system jet \(p_{\rm T}\) spectra, these correlations themselves offer a window to probe the initial stages of an event.

Within the precision of the \(A_{\rm J}\) and \(S\) measurements (and supported by concurrent jet mass distribution results), jet quenching is not observed in \(p\)+Au collisions at RHIC kinematics. The outlook to further advance the field with this data lies primarily in what constraints the measured jet/trigger-\(x_{\rm p}\) to EA correlations may apply to the initial stages of collisions. There is concurrent work at STAR looking at the long-range rapidity correlation of the activity within the \(\rm{EA}_{\rm TPC}\) to the rapidity of the triggers and jets, which will likely add an

Figure 7.1: Jet mass distribution based on jet substructure. [128]

other dimension to the observables. An important next step to actually motivate, constrain, or eliminate the physics cause(s) is to compare the performance of simulations with the encoded physics relative to these results. Such encoded physics may include those touched on in this thesis, including simple phase space constraints from energy conservation,4 a fluctuating proton through \(x_{\rm p}\) state configuration, possibly other cold nuclear matter effect(s), or even a more detailed cause from an IP Glasma simulation.

